WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.520
The first film built a Punnett square for Pp parents.

2
00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:08.040
Why might their real offspring counts miss its predicted ratio?

3
00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.300
A square gives each new plant a chance.

4
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.700
A real group records what happened after those separate chances played out.

5
00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.880
Think of the square as a chance map, not a packing list.

6
00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:28.920
For every new offspring, one box is selected by the parents’ allele contributions.

7
00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:31.900
Then the process starts again.

8
00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:37.080
Earlier flowers do not remove boxes or force later results.

9
00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.060
The map resets without remembering the last selection.

10
00:00:42.133 --> 00:00:44.153
Watch a small computed example.

11
00:00:45.573 --> 00:00:48.653
Several selectors land, and flowers fill the short row.

12
00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:51.633
The colors form an uneven group.

13
00:00:52.813 --> 00:00:54.393
That does not make the square wrong.

14
00:00:55.553 --> 00:00:58.833
A small group leaves plenty of room for chance to swing the count.

15
00:01:00.173 --> 00:01:02.073
Another short run could lean differently.

16
00:01:03.233 --> 00:01:05.633
The short row has leaned toward one color.

17
00:01:06.873 --> 00:01:08.393
The next slot is still empty.

18
00:01:09.733 --> 00:01:14.053
Does the earlier run force the next flower to balance the group, or does the same

19
00:01:14.053 --> 00:01:15.353
chance map apply again?

20
00:01:16.693 --> 00:01:17.693
Make your prediction.

21
00:01:23.233 --> 00:01:25.353
The same chance map applies again.

22
00:01:26.653 --> 00:01:30.573
The next result is not a correction chosen by the earlier results.

23
00:01:31.953 --> 00:01:33.673
Each offspring is a separate chance.

24
00:01:34.853 --> 00:01:39.813
The group may move nearer the prediction or farther away before later results add

25
00:01:39.813 --> 00:01:40.813
more evidence.

26
00:01:41.617 --> 00:01:43.457
Now extend the computed run.

27
00:01:44.777 --> 00:01:47.517
More flower slots stream in while the bars rescale.

28
00:01:48.777 --> 00:01:52.237
The observed share still wiggles, but its swings usually shrink.

29
00:01:53.557 --> 00:01:58.497
With more separate outcomes, an unusual short streak has less control over the whole

30
00:01:58.497 --> 00:02:03.597
group. The curve approaches its target unevenly, not in a straight march.

31
00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:08.310
Mendel tested the pattern with real pea plants.

32
00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:13.530
He counted offspring for several traits, not just one small family.

33
00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:18.730
Place each dominant-to-recessive ratio beside the square’s target.

34
00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:23.990
The rows gather near the target, but they do not land exactly together.

35
00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:27.520
That spread is the evidence to examine.

36
00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.900
A prediction is useful when many real results gather near it, even without an exact

37
00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:38.300
match. No single row must sit exactly on the target.

38
00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:43.860
In the lesson, you will compare counts, ratios and group sizes yourself.

39
00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:47.050
The square remains a one-gene model.

40
00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:52.830
Weather, soil, lost seeds, and more complex inheritance are outside it.

41
00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:57.350
Within its scope, it gives a clear target for repeated outcomes.

42
00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:03.250
Larger groups usually reveal that target more clearly than small groups do.
